from pathlib import Path
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2];p=P/'manuscript/manuscript.tex';s=p.read_text()
s=s.replace(r'\input{figs/revision_numbers.tex}',r'\input{figs/revision_numbers.tex}'+'\n'+r'\input{figs/science_numbers.tex}')
old='These results retain known means and group labels and provide mechanistic rather than real-data validation. These findings motivate three separate checks:'
new='A further held-out stress test shows that calibration-selected dispersion can improve marginal variance while worsening gene dependence, and can worsen energy distance when means are biased. A common-cell-type reanalysis retains the reversal of the conditional-versus-Poisson marker contrast between fixed-mean and end-to-end settings. These mechanistic tests and descriptive sensitivity analyses motivate three separate checks:'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
s=s.replace('so it applies to any of\nthese conditioning routes.','so the same diagnostic questions can be asked for each of\nthese conditioning routes, without assuming identical outcomes.')
block=r'''
\section{Scientific boundaries: held-out selection and biological support}
\label{sec:scientificboundaries}
\subsection{Design: separating calibration selection from evaluation}
The preceding estimated-dispersion experiment retains correct means and negative-binomial truth. We add a prespecified computational stress-test grid with 20 new seeds to examine two assumptions it leaves open: mean specification and residual gene dependence. ``Prespecified'' here means the grid and endpoints were fixed before these new runs; this is an exploratory revision analysis, not a prospective biological validation study. Each seed contains 12 synthetic datasets with six groups and 150 genes. Within each group, independently sampled training, validation, test, and reference sets contain 400, 200, 200, and 200 cells, respectively. Gene base rates follow the original gamma distribution, observed cell-size factors follow the original lognormal distribution, and group dispersion is log-uniform between 0.3 and 8.

Two generating families share conditional count means $\mu_{ig}$ and variances $\mu_{ig}+\mu_{ig}^{2}/\theta_k$. The first draws independent negative-binomial counts conditional on the mean. The second draws a shared residual multiplier $U_i$ with $\log U_i\sim\mathcal{N}(-v_k/2,v_k)$ and $v_k=\log(1+1/\theta_k)$, then draws conditionally independent Poisson counts with rates $\mu_{ig}U_i$. Consequently, $\mathbb{E}(U_i)=1$ and $\operatorname{Var}(U_i)=1/\theta_k$. The second family changes both marginal higher moments and residual dependence; it is not a pure covariance-only intervention. Its omitted multiplier induces conditional covariance $\mu_{ig}\mu_{ih}/\theta_k$ between distinct genes, which independent count draws at the supplied means cannot reproduce.

Within each family, predicted means are either correct, attenuate the observed within-group size variation by 75\%, or multiply alternating gene means by 0.6 and 1.4. The attenuation is $\widehat\mu_{ig}=\beta_{kg}\{1+0.25(\ell_i-1)\}$, where $\mu_{ig}=\beta_{kg}\ell_i$. This shrinks variation towards the baseline size; because the original lognormal size distribution has expectation greater than one, it also slightly changes the population mean. These are controlled perturbations using known base rates and observed size covariates, not fitted neural mean models. Group labels remain supplied.

We compare Poisson, pooled per-gene negative-binomial dispersion, and group-by-gene dispersion estimated from training residual moments using the predicted means and the same bounds as the preceding experiment. For each synthetic dataset, we choose the arm with the lowest median validation calibration error across its six groups; ties prefer Poisson, then pooling, then conditional dispersion. Test outcomes never enter this selection. The independent test endpoints are expressed-gene absolute log variance-ratio error, energy distance, median absolute gene-correlation error, and median absolute log$_2$ count-mean ratio error. Correlation error compares off-diagonal Pearson correlations of log expression on the 30 genes ranked highest by training mean counts; it measures magnitude error, not merely correlation-matrix rank agreement. The independent reference set estimates finite-sample disagreement between two draws from the same true distribution. For each endpoint, group values are collapsed to dataset medians and then to a seed median. All 120 family--mean--seed combinations are retained. Intervals describe the central 95\% of 20 seeds, not population confidence intervals; no new significance tests are introduced.

\subsection{Results: calibration can conceal dependence and mean errors}
With correct means and independent negative-binomial truth, validation selection reduces median test calibration error from \StressNBCorrectPoissonCal{} to \StressNBCorrectSelectedCal{}; its correlation error, \StressNBCorrectSelectedCorrelation{}, is close to the independent-reference value \StressNBCorrectReferenceCorrelation{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:scientificboundaries}). This is the favourable model-matched control. Under correlated truth with correct means, selection still improves calibration from \StressCorrCorrectPoissonCal{} to \StressCorrCorrectSelectedCal{} and energy distance from \StressCorrCorrectPoissonEnergy{} to \StressCorrCorrectSelectedEnergy{}. However, correlation error increases from \StressCorrCorrectPoissonCorrelation{} to \StressCorrCorrectSelectedCorrelation{}, compared with \StressCorrCorrectReferenceCorrelation{} between independent real draws. This deterioration occurs in all 20 seeds. Improvement in both marginal calibration and energy therefore does not establish recovery of gene dependence.

Under biased means and correlated truth, selected calibration error is \StressCorrBiasedSelectedCal{} versus \StressCorrBiasedPoissonCal{} for Poisson, but energy distance is \StressCorrBiasedSelectedEnergy{} versus \StressCorrBiasedPoissonEnergy{}. Calibration improves in every seed, whereas energy worsens in 18 of 20; the central 95\% interval of the paired energy difference still includes zero. The median count-mean error remains \StressCorrBiasedSelectedMean{}, well above the independent-reference value \StressCorrBiasedReferenceMean{}. Dispersion does not repair a biased expected mean. With attenuated size variation under independent negative-binomial truth, selection improves calibration from \StressNBAttenuatedPoissonCal{} to \StressNBAttenuatedSelectedCal{}, while correlation error increases from \StressNBAttenuatedPoissonCorrelation{} to \StressNBAttenuatedSelectedCorrelation{} in all seeds. Replacing missing structured variation with independent noise can therefore improve one endpoint at the expense of another.

The residual-moment estimator explains one source of ambiguity. For a predicted mean $m$ and true conditional mean $\mu$, both generating families satisfy
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\{(Y-m)^2-Y\mid\mu,m\}=\mu^2/\theta+(\mu-m)^2.
\end{equation}
Mean error contributes directly to the quantity attributed to dispersion. A variance deficit is consequently a diagnostic observation, not proof that independent observation noise is the missing mechanism. These tests establish specific counterexamples to broader interpretations of F4; they neither estimate their prevalence in real data nor identify a universal decision threshold.

\begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\fitfig{\input{figs/fig_scientificboundaries.tex}}
\caption{Held-out selection under mean and distribution misspecification. Rows combine independent negative-binomial (NB) or shared-lognormal Poisson truth with correct, attenuated, or gene-biased mean predictions. Training counts fit dispersion; independent validation counts choose among Poisson, pooled and conditional dispersion within each of 12 synthetic datasets. Test outcomes do not enter selection. Panels (a,b) show selected-minus-Poisson calibration and energy differences; negative favours selection. Panels (c,d) show absolute correlation and count-mean errors, with independent real draws as a sampling reference. Correlations use 30 training-ranked genes; mean errors use test-expressed genes. Small points show all 20 seeds, diamonds medians, and segments central 95\% seed intervals, not confidence intervals. Gene dependence can worsen despite improved calibration and energy.}
\label{fig:scientificboundaries}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Biological sensitivity: the same cell types across generation settings}
The original end-to-end marker readout scores a type only when at least 20 generated cells receive that label. The fixed-mean runs score 73 types, whereas end-to-end runs score 70--72 depending on arm and seed. To assess whether different scoring populations explain the narrowing marker contrast, we reanalyse the archived per-type Welch marker recalls on the intersection of scoreable types across all three arms and all seeds in both settings. This yields 69 common types. We retain each stage's three original seeds; independently fitted stages are not paired by seed index. This is a new descriptive analysis of existing results, not new model training or dataset-clustered evidence.

On common support, mean marker recall is \SupportFixedPois{} for Poisson, \SupportFixedGlobal{} for global dispersion and \SupportFixedCond{} for conditional dispersion in the fixed-mean setting (Fig.~\ref{fig:biologicalsupport}). In end-to-end generation, the corresponding values are \SupportEndPois{}, \SupportEndGlobal{} and \SupportEndCond{}. The conditional-minus-Poisson difference changes from \SupportFixedVsPois{} to \SupportEndVsPois{} and is negative in all three end-to-end fits. The conditional-minus-global difference changes from \SupportFixedVsGlobal{} to \SupportEndVsGlobal{}; its end-to-end sign varies across seeds. These stage summaries describe separately fitted settings, so their difference is not a causal estimate of the latent prior's contribution.

As a separate coverage sensitivity, assigning zero to an unscoreable type and averaging across the original 73 targets defines a coverage-adjusted composite, not the original marker-recall endpoint. It preserves the same direction of the conditional-minus-Poisson contrast. Thus, neither a common scoring population nor an explicit failure penalty restores the fixed-mean ranking in these end-to-end runs. The pooled common-support analysis does not overturn the original dataset-clustered null result for conditional versus Poisson marker recovery. It strengthens the boundary on translating a decoder-component finding into a full-generator recommendation.

\begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\fitfig{\input{figs/fig_biologicalsupport.tex}}
\caption{Marker recovery on a common scoring population. All arms and seeds are restricted to the same 69 cell types scoreable in both fixed-mean and end-to-end generation. Panel (a) shows mean recall of the 50 reference Welch-ranked marker genes per type. Panel (b) shows conditional-minus-comparator differences paired only within each stage and fit seed. Small circles are the three archived seed outcomes; diamonds are their means and segments their ranges, not confidence intervals. Fixed-mean and end-to-end seeds are independent. Conditional and Poisson draws share means within each setting; the end-to-end global arm comes from a separate fit. Common support retains the conditional-versus-Poisson reversal. These descriptive summaries do not establish dataset-clustered biological benefit.}
\label{fig:biologicalsupport}
\end{figure}

'''
assert r'\section{Discussion}' in s;s=s.replace(r'\section{Discussion}',block+r'\section{Discussion}',1)
s=s.replace('Biological harm from the tested global dispersion and benefit from adding conditional dispersion are therefore separate claims with different evidential strength.','Biological harm from the tested global dispersion and benefit from adding conditional dispersion are therefore separate claims with different evidential strength. Restricting marker evaluation to a common scoring population preserves the end-to-end reversal, so changes in which types are scoreable do not explain it away.')
s=s.replace('The diagnostic hierarchy matters.','The new held-out stress tests sharpen the scope of this distinction. A validation-selected arm can improve calibration while worsening dependence, and biased means can lead to conflicting calibration and energy verdicts. Residual moments mix true count variation with squared mean error. The appropriate response is to diagnose the mean and the missing structure before choosing a noise model; more conditional dispersion does not itself model residual covariance. The correlated generator deliberately changes both higher moments and dependence, so it supplies a counterexample to generality rather than a uniquely identified explanation of any real-data failure.\n\nThe diagnostic hierarchy matters.')
s=s.replace('misspecified mean functions, unknown groups, or full single-cell generators.','unknown groups, or full single-cell generators. Mean perturbations and a different generating family are examined separately below.')
s=s.replace('and a bounded moment estimator. Its synthetic datasets','and a bounded moment estimator. The additional stress tests relax correct means and the generating family through controlled perturbations, while retaining known base rates, supplied group labels and bounded moment estimation. Neither experiment validates learned neural means or amortized dispersion under these perturbations. Their synthetic datasets')
s=s.replace('First assess whether Poisson sampling on the fitted mean leaves a within-type variance deficit using expressed-gene calibration, with energy distance as a complementary diagnostic.','First assess mean adequacy and whether Poisson sampling on the fitted mean leaves a within-type variance deficit using expressed-gene calibration, with energy distance and gene dependence as complementary diagnostics.')
s=s.replace('Finally, evaluate marker recovery, classification, and complete-generator performance separately; improved calibration alone does not guarantee biological benefit.','Select candidate dispersion settings on separate validation data, then evaluate calibration, mean fidelity, dependence, marker recovery, classification, and complete-generator performance on held-out data. The validation-selected stress tests show why this separation prevents test-based selection but does not make calibration alone sufficient for biological benefit.')
